nodes.statement:
For any natural number $n\ge 4$, there exists explicitly computable $c_n\in~(0,1)$ such that for any $c\in (0,c_n)$ we have
  \begin{equation*}
      \dim_H FS_{n}(c)\geq n-2.
  \end{equation*}

Let $n\geq 4$. Then 
    we have
\begin{equation*}
    \dim_H FS_{n} \geq n-2,\,\qquad  \dim_H FS_{n,1}\geq n-2\, .
\end{equation*}

Let $m,n$ be positive integers satisfying
	\begin{equation} 
 	\tfrac{m}{n}\le \tfrac{1}{2}, 
 \qquad (m,n)\ne (1,3).

	\end{equation}
	Let $\Vert\cdot\Vert_1$ be any norm on $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\Vert\cdot\Vert_2$ be any 
	norm 
	on $\mathbb{R}^m$. Then there exists $c_{m,n}=c_{m,n}(\Vert\cdot\Vert_1,\Vert\cdot\Vert_2)>0$ such that for any $c\in[0,c_{m,n}]$ we have
 \begin{equation*}
 \dim_H FS_{m,n}(\Vert\cdot\Vert_1,\Vert\cdot\Vert_2,c) \geq (n-2)m.  
 \end{equation*}
 Analogous claims hold when we restrict to $\Omega\in FS_{m,n}(\Vert\cdot\Vert_1,\Vert\cdot\Vert_2,c)$ so that 
 \begin{itemize}
     \item[(i)]  $\Omega$
     has full $\mathbb{R}$-rank $m$
     \item[(ii)] the set of all its entries $\Omega_{i,j}$ together with $1$ is linearly independent over $\mathbb{Q}$.
 \end{itemize}
